y the people of Puerto Rico the  voters in Puerto Rico for that and a  simple majority in the House and the  Senate and the signature by the  president's of an enabling bill that's  it it's a bill what is there any  traction for that in on the mainland  side of the equation well yes yes indeed  and actually there was a bill that was  approved on the house side in Washington  last year the Senate did not act upon it  no so now we're back to square one as to  what are we going to do and I know we're  getting into a presidential race so  there may not be a lot of people wishing  to discuss this but we've been citizens  since nineteen seventeen we're proud of  it our men and women have served with  valor encouraging every single war ever  since then  and it is time for us to participate  fully in that decision-making process at  the national level you mentioned being  bilingual state essentially i'm sure it  puts you in an interesting position when  it comes to questions of immigration  porto rico had immigration to the  mainland in the 50s and i guess in the  70s in places like the northeast and  florida and also your part of the  country and you get a lot of immigrants  from places like Cuba and Haiti talk  about how that perspective makes you  different or the same than sort of  mainstream Republican approaches towards  immigration well certainly I i if you  want to call me something I'm a Reagan  Republican in the sense that I believe  that this country needs to keep growing  and opening trade and opening ourselves  to ideas and I believe we to open  ourselves in an orderly fashion legal  fashion to new people that want to work  hard I want to add to our national  fabric I want to do better for their own  families it has to be an orderly fashion  of course and that's what we have lacked  for the last few years but but I believe  that if we do it correctly we actually  receive other Cubans for example in the  60s and 70s and they added tremendously  to our economy in